  <p>Aruba's Carnival is one of the Caribbean's longest and most elaborate — a full season, not a weekend. If you're eyeing 2027, here is what's known, what gets confirmed when, and how to end up standing (or sitting on a balcony) in the right place.</p>
  <h2>The 2027 season at a glance</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Season:</strong> early January 2027 through the final parades before Ash Wednesday, which falls on <strong>10 February 2027</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Rhythm:</strong> weeks of jump-ups, the Lighting Parade at night, children's parades, and Grand Parades in San Nicolas and then Oranjestad as the finale.</li>
    <li><strong>The Oranjestad Grand Parade</strong> — the island's biggest single event — traditionally lands on the season's final Sunday, with the route through central Oranjestad including the waterfront boulevard.</li>
    <li><strong>What's confirmed when:</strong> the official calendar and route maps are published by the Carnival organisation in January. We update this page the day the 2027 route map lands.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Where to watch</h2>
  <p>The parade route through downtown is lined shoulder to shoulder hours before the first floats. The moves that work: claim shade early on the boulevard side, bring water (February sun is strong even at 27°C), and know where your bathroom is — this is a long, glorious afternoon. The luxury version is a balcony on the route: you watch from above, retreat to air conditioning, and your drinks stay cold. Casa Playa stands on L.G. Smith Boulevard, the waterfront street the parade tradition runs along — which is exactly why our Carnival dates sell between November and January.</p>
  <h2>Booking math for Carnival travellers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Accommodation near the route books out in the <strong>eight to ten weeks after November's date confirmation</strong>.</li>
    <li>Flights from the US and Argentina (Aruba's fastest-growing Carnival market) are at seasonal peak — book them with the room.</li>
    <li>Aruba is outside the hurricane belt and prices in US dollars: a Carnival trip planned a year out carries no weather asterisk and no currency surprise.</li>
  </ul>
